\[
V_{\text{final}} = V_{\text{sample}} + V_{\text{standard}} = 0.100 \, \text{L} + 0.0100 \, \text{L} = 0.110 \, \text{L}
\]

Now, we can use mass balance to find the concentration of the compound in the original drinking water sample. The mass balance equation is:

\[
C_{\text{standard}} \times V_{\text{standard}} + C_{\text{sample}} \times V_{\text{standard}} = C_{\text{final}} \times V_{\text{final}}
\]

Substitute the known values:

\[
0.0100 \, \text{M} \times 0.0100 \, \text{L} + C_{\text{sample}} \times 0.100 \, \text{L} = 0.0125 \, \text{M} \times 0.110 \, \text{L}
\]

Solving for \( C_{\text{sample}} \):

\[
0.000100 \, \text{mol} + 0.100 \, \text{L} \times C_{\text{sample}} = 0.001355 \, \text{mol}
\]

\[
0.100 \, \text{L} \times C_{\text{standard}} = 0.001355 \, \text{mol} - 0.000100 \, \text{mol}
\]

\[
0.100 \, \text{L} \times C_{\text{standard}} = 0.001255 \, \text{mol}
\]

\[
C_{\text{standard}} = \frac{0.001255 \, \text{mol}}{0.100 \, \text{L}} = 0.01255 \, \text{M}
