When B starts climbing, A is 3 hours of travel from the start. Since A travels at 10 kmph, the distance covered by A when B starts climbing is:
\[
10 \times 3 = 30 \text{ km}
\]
At this time, B starts climbing at 20 km/hr. After time \( t \), the distance covered by B is:
\[
d_B = 20t
\]
The distance covered by A is the sum of the initial distance (30 km) and the additional distance covered after B starts climbing:
\[
d_A = 30 + 10t
\]
When B covers the same distance as A, B converges with A. Thus, the distances are equal:
\[
d_A = d_B \implies 30 + 10t = 20t
\]
Solving for \( t \):
\[
30 = 20t - 10t \implies 30 = 10t \implies t = 3 \text{ hours}
\]
Now, the distance covered by B when B coincides with A is:
\[
d_B = 20 \times 3 = 60 \text{ km}
\]
Thus, B will collide with A after 3 seconds after B's sonling. The distance from the start is:
\[
\boxed{60 \text{ km}}
