","To calculate the average radiation dose to the liver, we need to use the formula for the radiation dose:

Dose (in mJ/cm³) = Energy (in J) / Mass (in kg)

First, we need to convert the mass of the liver tissue to kg:

Mass = 1.6 kg

Next, we need to convert the energy from J to J/kg:

Energy per kg = 0.12 J / 1.6 kg = 0.075 J/kg

Next, we need to convert the energy per kg to mJ/cm³. To do this, we need to know the density of the liver tissue. The density of the liver tissue is approximately 1.1 g/cm³, which is 1.1 kg/m³. To convert this to kg/cm³, we divide by 1000:

1.1 kg/m³ = 0.0011 kg/cm³

Now, we can calculate the dose in J/cm³:

Dose (in J/cm³) = 0.075 J/kg * 1.1 kg/cm³ = 0.0825 J/cm³

To convert the dose from J/cm³ to mJ/cm³, we multiply by 1000:

Dose (in mJ/cm³) = 0.0825 J/cm³ * 1000 = 82.5 mJ/cm³

So, the average radiation dose to the liver is 82.5 mJ/cm³.",relevance,"# Task Description
