For a mole of \(^4\text{He}\) at 1 bar and 290 K, determine the thermal de Broglie wavelength \(\lambda\), the fugacity \(\exp(\mu/kT)\), the free enthalpy (in J), and the entropy (in J/K). Here, helium may be taken as an ideal gas.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","For a mole of \(^4\text{He}\) at 1 bar and 290 K, we are given the following parameters:
- \( n = 1 \) mole (moles)
- \( p = 1 \) bar = \( 10^5 \) Pa (pressure)
- \( T = 290 \) K (temperature)

Assuming helium is an ideal gas, we can use the ideal gas law to find the volume:
\[
V = \frac{nRT}{p} = \frac{1 \times 8.314 \text{ J/mol·K} \times 290 \text{ K}}{10^5 \text{ Pa}} = 2.40 \text{ L} = 2.40 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}^3
\]

#### Step 1: Thermal de Broglie wavelength \(\lambda\)
The thermal de Broglie wavelength is given by:
\[
\lambda = \frac{h}{m\sqrt{2k_BT}}
\]
For \(^4\text{He}\):
- \( m = 4 \times 1.60 \times 10^{-27} \text{ kg} = 6.4 \times 10^{-27} \text{ kg} \) (mass of one helium atom)
- \( h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J·s} \) (Planck's constant)
- \( k_B = 1.38 \times 10^{-23} \text{ J/K} \) (Boltzmann constant)

\[
\lambda = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{6.4 \times 10^{-27} \sqrt{2 \times 1.38 \times 10^{-23} \times 290}} = 1.0 \times 10^{-10} \text{ m} = 1 \text{ nm}
\]

### Step 2: Fugacity \(\exp(\mu/kT)\)
For an ideal gas, the fugacity is equal to 1. Therefore:
\[
\boxed{\exp(\mu/kT} = 1
\]

### Step 3: Free Helmholtz Free Energy (in J)
The Helmholtz free energy for an ideal gas is:
\[
G = nRT \ln \left( \frac{RT}{p} \right) = nRT \ln V)
\]
Substituting the values:
\[
G = 1 \times 8.314 \times 290 \times \ln (2.40 \times 10^{-3}) = 2410 \times (-2.20 \text{ atm}) = 5380 \text{ J}
\]

### Step 4: Entropy (in J/K)
The entropy for an ideal gas is:
\[
S = nR \ln \left( \frac{RT}{p} \right) = nR \ln V
\]
Substituting the values:
\[
S = 1 \times 8.314 \times \ln (2.40 \times 10^{-3}) = 8.314 \times (-2.20) = -18.6 \text{ J/K}
\]

### Final Answers:
