## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
A cylinder contains 2 moles of an ideal gas at an initial pressure of 2 atm and an initial volume of 4L. The gas expands isothermally until its volume becomes 8L. Calculate the work done by the gas during the expansion process.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
For an isothermal process, the work done by the gas is given by:
\[
W = nRT \ln \left( \frac{V_f}{V_i} \right)
\]
where:
- \( V_f = 8 \, \text{L} \) (final volume)
- \( V_i = 4 \, \text{L} \) (initial volume)
- \( n = 2 \, \text{moles} \) (number of moles)
- \( T = 298 \, \text{K} \) (temperature, assuming \( T = 298 \, \text{K} \) since the process is isothermal)
- \( R = 0.0821 \, \text{L·atm·K}^{-1} \, \text{mol}^{-1} \) (gas constant)

Substituting the values:
\[
W = 2 \times 0.0821 \times 298 \times \ln \left( \frac{8}{4} \right)
\]
\[
W = 4.856 \times \ln 2
\]
\[
W \approx 4.856 \times 0.6930
\]
\[
W \approx 3.32 \, \text{J}
\]

Thus, the work done by the gas during the expansion process is \(\boxed{3.32 \, \text{J}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

