A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The force acting on the proton is given by \( F = qE \), where \( q \) is the charge and \( E \) is the electric field strength.  
\[
F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}) \times (500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}.
\]
The acceleration of the proton is given by \( a = \frac{F}{m} \), where \( m \) is the mass.  
\[
a = \frac{8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.74 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2.
\]
The acceleration of the proton is \(\boxed{4.74 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).

(b) The acceleration is constant, so the velocity of the proton is given by \( v = at \), where \( t \) is the time.  
\[
t = \frac{v}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{4.74 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.35 \times 10^{-14} \, \text{s}.
\]
The time it takes for the proton to reach a speed of \( 35,000 \, \text{m/s} \) is \(\boxed{7.35 \times 10^{-14} \, \text{s}}\).
