","The intensity of thermal radiation of a black body is given by the Planck equation, which relates the intensity \(I(\lambda,T)\) to the wavelength \( \lambda \) and temperature \( T \). The equation is:  
\[
I(\lambda,T) = \frac{1}{hc^2} \frac{1}{1 - e^{-hc/kT}} \cdot e^{-hc/kT \lambda}
\]  
where:  
- \( I(\lambda, T) \) is the intensity of the thermal radiation at wavelength \( \lambda \) and temperature \( T \).  
- \( h \) is Planck's constant (\( 6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J} \cdot \text{s} \)).  
-\( c \) is the speed of light (\( 3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s} \)).  
- \( \lambda \) is the wavelength of the radiation.  
- \( T \) is the temperature of the black body in Kelvin.  
- \( c \lambda \) is the speed of light multiplied by the wavelength, which is inversely proportional to the frequency \( \nu \).  
- \( k \) is the Boltzmann constant (\( 1.38 \times 10^{-23} \, \text{J/K} \)).  

