","To solve this problem, we will use the Engineering Elements Elements (EES) software. The E-tables for the psychrometric properties of air are available in EES.

The dry-bulb temperature (T_dry) = 24°C
The wet-bulb temperature (T_vap) = 17°C
 The atmospheric pressure (P) = 1 atm = 101.325 kPa
 The latent heat of vaporization (L_fg) = 24.5 kJ/kg
 the specific heat of air (Cp) = 1.02 kJ/kg·K

#### Part (a): Specific Humidity (y)
The specific humidity is the ratio of the mass of water vapor to the mass of dry air. We can calculate this using the psychrometric relations.

In EES, we can use the following equation:

\[
y = \frac{L_fg (T_vap - T_d)}{P_p}
\]

where \( P_p \) is the partial pressure of water vapor.

The psychrometric equation for the wet-bulb temperature is:

\[
T_v = T_d + \frac{L_fg}{c_p} \left( \frac{P_p}{P - P_p} \right)
\]

Solving for \( P_p \):

\[
P_p = \frac{P \left( T_v - T_d \right)}{1 + \frac{L_f}{c_p} \left( \frac{T_v - T_d}{P} \right)}
\]

Substituting the values:

\[
P_p = \frac{101.325 \times (17 - 24)}{1 + \frac{24.5}{1.02} \left( \frac{17 - 24}{101.325} \right)} = 0.0010 \text{ Pa}
\]

The partial pressure of water vapor is approximately 0.0010 psia (1.02 mm Hg).

The specific humidity is:

\[
y = \frac{P_p}{P - P_p} = \frac{0.0010}{1.01325 - 0.0010} = 0.000985 \text{ kg/kg dry air}
\]

#### Part (b): Enthalpy (h)
The enthalpy per kg of dry air is given by:

\[
h = c_p T_d + L_fg w
\]

where \( w = 0.000985 \text{ kg/kg dry air} \).

\[
h = (1.02 \times 24) + 24.5 \times 0.000985 = 24.48 + 0.0241 = 24.50 \text{ kJ/kg dry air}
\]

